The same idea with the divisor **1024** (the note of question 7 says 1 KB = 1024 B): whole kilobytes = quotient of `num` by 1024, remaining bytes = remainder.

**C:**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
  int kb, b, num;
  printf("Enter number of bytes: ");
  scanf("%d", &num);
  kb = num / 1024;
  b = num % 1024;
  printf("%dB is equivalent to %dKB and %dB\n", num, kb, b);
  char c = getchar();
  return 0;
}
```

**Pascal:**

```pascal
program Bytes;
var kb, b, num: integer;
BEGIN
    write('Enter number of bytes: ');
    readln(num);
    kb := num div 1024;
    b := num mod 1024;
    writeln(num, ' B is equivalent to ', kb, 'KB and ', b, 'B');
    readln;
END.
```

Changes made: the prompt text (bytes instead of centimetres), the variable names (`kb`, `b`), the divisor (`1024` instead of `100`) and the message. Compile and correct until the compiler reports no error. (The code was not compiled: its logic was checked with a Python simulation, see the notes of the paper.) One mark for the correct division and remainder by 1024, one mark for the adapted input and output.
